AB  - AI-powered code generation tools are increasingly embedded in Agile software development, accelerating routine tasks such as scaffolding, documentation, and test creation. Despite productivity benefits, concerns remain regarding code quality, security, and developer trust. This study investigates how AI-generated code affects maintainability, readability, and vulnerability exposure in Agile environments. A mixed-methods design was employed, combining a developer survey, static analysis of public repositories, and interviews with Agile team leads. Results show that AI tools reduce cognitive load and speed up development but introduce higher issue density and lower readability compared to human-written code. Developers demonstrate calibrated skepticism, performing extensive reviews of AI outputs. The study proposes the Artificial Intelligence Integration Maturity Model and empirically validates the AI-Generated Code Trust Scale, demonstrating how trust calibration aligns with organizational maturity. Findings support structured human-in-the-loop review, AI-specific CI/CD gates, and prompt engineering training as key practices for responsible AI integration. The research provides essential knowledge about how automation systems should be managed to protect software quality in contemporary engineering operations.
ER  - 

