AB  - Decision pipelines in massive data ecosystems require automobile governance model that is capable of incorporating integrity, compliance, and operational agility across diverse data sources. Conventional Data Governance models typically do not have a continuous tracking, traceability and dynamically adaptive controls that are needed in dynamic big data setups. In this paper, a DataOps-based Big Data Governance framework that synthesizes policy-based metadata control, federated access control, real-time data quality measurements, and real-time compliance orchestration is presented. This proposed architecture makes use of DataOps practices, including versioned data engineering, automated validation, lineage tracking, and policy enforcement through CI/CD, to promote end-to-end transparency and reliability in decision pipelines. Anomaly detection that is built on machine learning improves proactive governance as it predicts anomalies, finds inconsistencies, and removes security risk even before deployment. The outcomes of experimental work on multi-source enterprise data reveal that the scores of data quality, the reduction of latency, the accuracy of lineage, and compliance with policies have improved. The framework facilitates scalable, secure, and explainable analytics, as it consolidates governance goals and operational automation, thereby speeding up reliable decision intelligence in the current big data systems.
ER  - 

