AB  - Estimating technical debt (TD) is crucial in managing large-scale software projects. This study introduces a machine learning-based framework for TD estimation in opensource Java projects, integrating code complexity metrics and developer interaction features. Using a dataset of 55 million Java files from 100,000 GitHub repositories, we apply XGBoost, LSTM, and Informer models for multi-horizon TD forecasting. SHAP (SHapley Additive Explanations) is utilized for interpretability, revealing key TD drivers such as cyclomatic complexity, commit frequency, and review cycle time. The results demonstrate that the Informer model outperforms others, achieving an $R^{2}$ of 0.9250 and an MAE of 3.60, particularly excelling in long-term forecasting. This model's ability to predict TD with high accuracy makes it suitable for integration into CI/CD pipelines, ensuring proactive TD management. The proposed framework provides actionable insights for developers, enabling them to prioritize remediation based on the most significant factors contributing to TD. This research offers a scalable, data-driven solution for software maintenance, contributing to improved software quality and sustainability.
ER  - 

